Object.freeze is shallow. It freezes only the immediate object—assigning to top-level properties fails. Nested objects are unfrozen, so mutations like `cfg.db.host = 'y'` succeed.

To lock the whole structure:
```javascript
const deepFreeze = (obj) => {
  Object.freeze(obj);
  Object.getOwnPropertyNames(obj).forEach(prop => {
    if (obj[prop] !== null && (typeof obj[prop] === "object" || typeof obj[prop] === "function")) {
      deepFreeze(obj[prop]);
    }
  });
  return obj;
};

const cfg = deepFreeze({ db: { host: 'x' } });
cfg.db.host = 'y'; // now fails silently (or throws in strict mode)
```

The assignment to `cfg.db` would still fail. But `cfg.db.host` only fails if you recursively freeze all reachable objects.

`defer` runs when the enclosing *function* returns, not at block scope. Every `Close()` in that loop defers until the function exits, so all file handles stay open simultaneously. You hit the OS file descriptor limit.

Fix: either close explicitly in the loop, or move the loop into its own function:

```go
func processFile(f string) error {
    fh, err := os.Open(f)
    if err != nil {
        return err
    }
    defer fh.Close()
    // work with fh
    return nil
}

for _, f := range files {
    processFile(f)
}
```

Now each `defer` fires when `processFile` returns, freeing the descriptor before the next iteration.
